    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        Enter Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code into Missouri sales tax and the answer comes back as Sales tax. There is no hidden constant here: change sales tax and purchase amount moves with it.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the Missouri sales tax calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship behind Missouri sales tax: it takes 2 numbers and reports Sales tax.</p><p>Written with the labels from the form, Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code).</p><p>The answers are Sales tax; they are reported together because a single calculation usually settles more than one question.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Fill in 2 boxes and the page has everything it needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li></ul><p>What you get back is Sales tax, shown next to the form rather than on a page of its own.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>As far as Sales tax is concerned the numbers are unitless — it neither converts nor guesses a unit, so enter every value in the same scale you intend to read the answer in.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The relationship is shown here as the page solves it, with the form&#039;s labels standing where the variable names would.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> = <code>get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code)</code></li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship on this page, so any answer can be checked by putting it back into the box that owns it and seeing whether the remaining ones agree.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> Sales tax is a result, and each can be typed into. Putting a past answer back into one of them turns the calculation round and solves for whichever box is then empty — useful, but only if that is what you meant to do.</li><li><strong>Purchase amount left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Purchase amount is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Purchase location ZIP Code is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Two numbers measured in different units, or one taken at the start of a season and one at the end, are the commonest source of an answer that is internally consistent and still wrong. Convert Purchase amount to one scale before entering anything.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The boxes on the Missouri sales tax form, one at a time: what each one wants, how it is measured, and what the page does if you leave it empty.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase amount is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. It feeds Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase location ZIP Code is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li></ul><p>Every one of those boxes is optional in a sense: fill all but one and the page solves for the one you left. It is only when two are blank that the page has nothing to choose between.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Each box below is a different question about the same calculation. Read them in this order:</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> is a plain number. It is computed from Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code, so it changes the moment any of them does. Compare it with the defaults above before trusting it. A figure far from that range is worth checking against a hand calculation. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Getting an answer, step by step', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ol><li><strong>Collect the inputs.</strong> Have Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code ready and all in one unit. The page will convert whatever a chip says it is; it cannot tell that one of the numbers was measured in something else.</li><li><strong>Enter them and press Calculate.</strong> Fill the form — starting with Purchase amount — and press Calculate. The panel updates in place, and the fields you typed into keep your numbers.</li><li><strong>Read the answer against the defaults.</strong> Check Sales tax against the worked example above. If the two disagree by a round factor, the chip or the decimal separator is the place to look first.</li><li><strong>Change one box to check your understanding.</strong> Double one input, leaving the rest alone, and press Calculate again. The direction the answer moves tells you whether that box sits in the numerator or the denominator — and whether the number you got a moment ago was plausible.</li></ol><p>That is the whole procedure. Everything else on this page is the reasoning behind it.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Limits worth knowing', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>It scales in a straight line.</strong> The relationship behind Sales tax is a straight-line one: proportionally more input gives proportionally more output, which is what makes the sensitivity table above a set of constants rather than a curve.</li><li><strong>It is a formula, not a model of your case.</strong> The page assumes Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code are measured, independent and in the unit the chip says. It has no way to know that one of them was estimated, that two were taken at different times of day, or that the relationship shifts at the extremes of its range.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where this calculation comes up', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The typical use is one direction of the relationship: Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code measured, Sales tax wanted.</p><p>It is worth having the calculation written down even when you only need the answer once: an entered figure can be checked, and a remembered one cannot.</p><p>If the answer you need is a step further on, the nearest pages are <a href="/finance/grp">Gross rating point (GRP)</a>, <a href="/finance/gross-rent-multiplier">Gross rent multiplier Calculator</a> and <a href="/finance/gross-to-net">Gross to net Calculator</a>.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'About this page', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Missouri sales tax is a calculator, not an article with a form attached: the numbers in the panel come from the formulas on this page, applied to whatever is in the boxes, and nothing is looked up in a table.</p><p>Its own defaults are run through its own formulas every time the page is built, so the examples above are not illustrations: if the relationship changed, the numbers in the article would change with it, and a page whose arithmetic stopped agreeing with its text would fail the check rather than quietly drift.</p><p>What the page keeps is nothing. The calculation is unchanged between visits. has no account, no history and no server-side record of what you typed; the fields hold your numbers for as long as the tab is open, and the share button builds a link from them only if you ask it to.</p><p>It belongs to the same catalogue as the rest of the site, and follows the same rule as all of them: the formula is printed, the working is shown, and the answer can be checked by hand if you want to.</p><p>The formula is printed in full above precisely so that the page can be argued with. Compare it with the reference you are working from; if they differ, the difference will be in a constant or a unit, and both are visible in the text.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where this fits among the other calculators', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>A criterion for choosing between these pages: Missouri sales tax answers a different question from the one above, even where the two share a sentence of explanation.</p><p>The nearest are <a href="/finance/grp">Gross rating point (GRP)</a>, <a href="/finance/gross-rent-multiplier">Gross rent multiplier Calculator</a> and <a href="/finance/gross-to-net">Gross to net Calculator</a>. Each takes its own inputs and reports its own answer, and none of them shares a stored value with this page — open two in separate tabs and they will not interfere.</p><p>If the value you need is not the one reported here but a step before or after it, one of those pages probably reports it directly rather than making you convert by hand.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Putting the answer to work', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>An answer from Missouri sales tax is a number to act on, so the useful question is what to do with Sales tax once the panel shows it.</p><ul><li><strong>Record the inputs with the answer.</strong> A number on its own is not reproducible. Write down Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code alongside it — a week later the answer is checkable and the reasoning is not.</li><li><strong>Re-run it when a measurement changes.</strong> The page keeps nothing between visits, which is a feature: every figure is worked out from the numbers in front of it, so updating one input and pressing Calculate again gives a fresh answer rather than an average of the old and the new.</li><li><strong>Keep the formula with the figure.</strong> A number from a page like this one travels better than a number from a spreadsheet cell, because the formula that produced it is visible on the same page and does not depend on a file being kept.</li><li><strong>Compare rather than convert.</strong> Run it once for the case in front of you and once for a case you understand well. The gap between the two answers tells you more about your own situation than either number does on its own.</li></ul><p>The page is a calculator, not a system of record. Copy what you need out of it, keep it with the inputs that produced it, and come back with different numbers whenever the inputs change.</p>
            TXT],
        ],
        'faq' => [
            ['q' => 'What does the Missouri sales tax calculator work out?', 'a' => 'Sales tax, from Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code. The relationship is Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code).'],
            ['q' => 'Which values do I need before I start?', 'a' => <<<'TXT'
            Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code. With purchase amount and purchase location ZIP Code in place, Sales tax can stay blank and the page will work it out.
            TXT],
            ['q' => 'How precise is the result?', 'a' => <<<'TXT'
            Sales tax is computed at full precision and rounded once at the end, so what you read is a view of the number the formula produced rather than a second calculation from a rounded one.
            TXT],
            ['q' => 'What happens if I leave a box empty?', 'a' => 'The page needs every box filled: Sales tax is fixed as the value it works out, so there is nothing to solve for when an input is missing.'],
            ['q' => 'Does it round to the nearest whole number?', 'a' => 'It rounds for display only; the underlying value of Sales tax keeps more digits than the panel prints.'],
        ],
